WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:19.229 --> 00:00:21.650
Current trends are very clear.

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:25.000
First of all, it's easier and and
easier for companies to gather data.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.660
So, there's this big data movement and

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:29.700
that doesn't appear to be
slowing down anytime soon.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.330
Number two, currently,
advertisers can already engage in

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:38.244
personalized advertising or
dynamic creatives.

00:00:38.244 --> 00:00:40.282
The underlying idea is that well,

00:00:40.282 --> 00:00:45.004
since I know some information about this
consumer, what he or she likes to buy?

00:00:45.004 --> 00:00:47.570
What types of preferences they have?

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:51.140
Maybe this is based on browsing history,
maybe purchase history, maybe zip code.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:52.950
All these things
are observable online today.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.755
Why don't I use that information
to cater my message,

00:00:56.755 --> 00:00:59.144
to tailor my message to
that particular user?

00:00:59.144 --> 00:01:02.508
And so, we wanted to understand
where are we headed.

00:01:02.508 --> 00:01:04.651
Where we will be in say, ten years time?

00:01:04.651 --> 00:01:09.443
[MUSIC]

00:01:09.443 --> 00:01:12.046
There's a reason that advertisers
will do what they're doing and

00:01:12.046 --> 00:01:14.410
there's a reason consumers will buy or
not.

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:18.248
And so, game theory allows us to
incorporate consumer incentives and

00:01:18.248 --> 00:01:20.663
advertiser's incentives into the model.

00:01:20.663 --> 00:01:24.689
First of all, the advertiser can
collect information about consumer,

00:01:24.689 --> 00:01:28.781
then the advertiser can send a message
that can rely on that information or

00:01:28.781 --> 00:01:32.554
not and the consumer can decide
whether to buy or not, for example.

00:01:32.554 --> 00:01:33.871
So this could be anything,

00:01:33.871 --> 00:01:37.150
could be a dealership trying to
attract a consumer for a test drive.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:42.320
It could be a realtor trying to attract
person to visit an open house or it could

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:47.262
be an online app trying to get me to
the store and try a new pair of sneakers.

00:01:47.262 --> 00:01:52.042
The first thing that were identifying and
exploring is the fact that by

00:01:52.042 --> 00:01:56.508
giving information,
we're lending ourselves to be targeted.

00:01:56.508 --> 00:02:02.123
And in a sense, that information can
be used against us by the other party.

00:02:02.123 --> 00:02:05.636
[MUSIC]

00:02:05.636 --> 00:02:08.863
A big question in marketing is
that of truth in advertising.

00:02:08.863 --> 00:02:10.210
Now, that's central to marketing.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:13.653
Can we trust sellers and what they claim?

00:02:13.653 --> 00:02:18.403
Even the Federal Trade Commission
allows advertisers to engage in what

00:02:18.403 --> 00:02:22.362
they call persuasive puffery
to puff up their attributes,

00:02:22.362 --> 00:02:25.608
to claim that they have
the best burgers in town or

00:02:25.608 --> 00:02:29.584
that they have the most trendy shirts or
whatever you want.

00:02:29.584 --> 00:02:31.598
When in reality,
this is always a little hard to judge.

00:02:31.598 --> 00:02:32.727
These are subjective claims.

00:02:32.727 --> 00:02:37.383
But if they're sort of reasonably
sort of expected claims,

00:02:37.383 --> 00:02:42.150
expected exaggerations, well,
that turns out to be legal.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:44.980
That creates a very interesting
interaction with personalized advertising.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:50.625
Because if I have that leeway to convey
the exact attributes that you care about,

00:02:50.625 --> 00:02:55.870
I can customize my message to make it
the most appealing to you as possible.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:58.935
At the same time as consumers
understand that this is happening,

00:02:58.935 --> 00:03:01.174
that's the genesis of
the credibility issue.

00:03:01.174 --> 00:03:04.867
They understand that the ads have
been tailored to their tastes.

00:03:04.867 --> 00:03:10.057
And so now, that creates a mistrust
between consumers and advertisers.

00:03:10.057 --> 00:03:14.465
They understand that advertisers have
that leeway to customize their message,

00:03:14.465 --> 00:03:16.748
to make it the most attractive possible.

00:03:16.748 --> 00:03:23.019
And so, sometimes reported low
credibility that advertisers already

00:03:23.019 --> 00:03:28.670
have with consumers is in peril
of becoming even less credible.

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:31.285
[MUSIC]

00:03:31.285 --> 00:03:35.975
So in an age where data is almost free and
it's becoming so easy to collect data,

00:03:35.975 --> 00:03:39.279
how should we think about what is best for
advertisers?

00:03:39.279 --> 00:03:40.616
Should they get better data or not?

00:03:40.616 --> 00:03:42.550
And what is best for consumers?

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:47.175
For companies, best practices are to
always inform customers of the data that

00:03:47.175 --> 00:03:51.677
they are collecting about consumers and
that is a good thing for companies.

00:03:51.677 --> 00:03:54.857
We usually think,
why would companies want to disclose that?

00:03:54.857 --> 00:03:59.044
Well, it turns out consumers will
appreciate the fact that there's clear

00:03:59.044 --> 00:04:02.709
privacy policies and
there's clear data disclosure policies.

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:05.167
And companies like Google, Facebook,

00:04:05.167 --> 00:04:07.842
Amazon now have that
type of information and

00:04:07.842 --> 00:04:12.700
you can access your personal information
that they're collecting about you.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:14.801
[MUSIC]

00:04:14.801 --> 00:04:16.102
On the consumer front,

00:04:16.102 --> 00:04:19.622
should I share my personal
information with advertiser or not?

00:04:19.622 --> 00:04:21.285
Well, it turns out we
find a falling effect.

00:04:21.285 --> 00:04:26.300
If these are mainstream preferences like,
I would like a spacious house or

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:30.614
a family friendly car,
those sort of mainstream preferences.

00:04:30.614 --> 00:04:31.834
Most people have those preferences.

00:04:31.834 --> 00:04:37.497
It turns out, we don't benefit quite
a lot from giving off that information.

00:04:37.497 --> 00:04:43.142
If anything, we can get sales people
now pitching us back our tastes.

00:04:43.142 --> 00:04:45.920
However, if our tastes are very specific,

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:51.037
if there's something we would really like
to find or we would like to be found by.

00:04:51.037 --> 00:04:53.870
So maybe I like this particular
type genre of comic book or

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:56.703
this particular type of music
that very few people like or

00:04:56.703 --> 00:05:00.792
there's very few artists playing, then I
would like to be found by those people.

00:05:00.792 --> 00:05:06.504
And so those are the situations where I
would like to give off information and

00:05:06.504 --> 00:05:11.948
disclose my likes and dislikes are in
settings where my tastes are really

00:05:11.948 --> 00:05:17.234
not representative, but
they're really sort of niche type tastes.

00:05:17.234 --> 00:05:20.840
[MUSIC]

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:24.164
In a sense, an ironic finding
of our paper is that better

00:05:24.164 --> 00:05:28.567
information can be worse for
advertisers, which is really surprising.

00:05:28.567 --> 00:05:29.618
It's a perverse effect.

00:05:29.618 --> 00:05:31.475
I get better information about consumers.

00:05:31.475 --> 00:05:36.127
This is all what managers talk about,
sort of this big data revolution and

00:05:36.127 --> 00:05:37.636
I can become worse off.

00:05:37.636 --> 00:05:42.503
Because consumers are understanding
that firms are taking advantage

00:05:42.503 --> 00:05:46.875
of this data to personalize their
messages, to target them and

00:05:46.875 --> 00:05:51.671
they're not likely to respond in
a positive way to these type of ads.

00:05:51.671 --> 00:05:54.020
And consumers and firms, do we have
only two ways to build about this.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:58.253
And particular managers can either
decide not to gather to much data about

00:05:58.253 --> 00:06:00.444
consumers, which is hard to impose.

00:06:00.444 --> 00:06:04.708
Or at the very least,
disclose how those that are being used.

00:06:04.708 --> 00:06:08.524
And so if I'm seeing an ad,
if I'm targeted with an ad,

00:06:08.524 --> 00:06:11.635
I would like to know why
I'm getting this ad.

00:06:11.635 --> 00:06:13.510
Getting rid of that uncertainty for
consumers is useful.

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:14.970
Because now, they can make the call.

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:15.851
They can understand.

00:06:15.851 --> 00:06:17.966
Well, okay,
this firm has this data about me.

00:06:17.966 --> 00:06:20.762
They want to recommend me this product,
but

00:06:20.762 --> 00:06:25.459
it's up to me whether I want to pay
attention to that call to action or not.

00:06:25.459 --> 00:06:28.909
[MUSIC]

